WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:10.670
We had it.
From January 21, 1976 to

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:14.429
October 24, 2003,
we had a commercial supersonic passenger plane

00:00:14.429 --> 00:00:19.800
called Concorde.
Today it takes 7 hours to fly from New York

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:25.950
to London. On the Concorde it took
About 3:30 (just under 3 hours if it was record

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.130
time).
A journey that would have taken the Titanic

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:32.050
137 hours had become just barely long enough
to

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:36.879
Watch Titanic while crossing the Atlantic.
The Concorde came to represent class,

00:00:36.879 --> 00:00:39.140
style,
and the miracle of engineering.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:43.890
Here’s a normal plane landing and here’s
the Concorde landing. Which one are you looking

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:47.420
at?
With Concorde, we all looked up and pointed.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:53.859
And then in 2003… it stopped.
We had commercial supersonic flight and just

00:00:53.859 --> 00:00:58.350
let it go.
Why? Why did the Concorde become a museum

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:02.489
exhibit?
This is the Smithsonian’s Concorde, and

00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:07.750
the curator who got it flew on it, too.
“I did see the color of the sky at 60,000

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:11.700
feet. It’s this most gorgeous deep, deep
purple.”

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:17.049
How did a breakthrough become a piece of memorabilia?
The answer says something about how innovation

00:01:17.049 --> 00:01:19.600
really sticks. And it’s complicated.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:25.280
“I’ve got a personal interest in the SST,
and I’d like to tell you about it.”

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:31.180
SST equals Supersonic transport, any transportation
that’s faster than the speed of sound. It

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:34.159
became a dream
after Chuck Yeager broke the sound barrier

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:39.049
in 1947,
And a technological race in the 1950s and

00:01:39.049 --> 00:01:42.890
1960s, combined
Cold War competition with a classic mid-century

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:47.590
faith in engineering. Americans, Russians,
and the British and French governments dumped

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:51.619
hundreds of millions of dollars into supersonic
R&amp;D.

00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:55.429
(Think rooms full of engineers in short sleeves
and ties.)

00:01:55.429 --> 00:02:01.350
The then Seattle-based Boeing won the American
design contract in 1967, (that’s where a

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.479
certain basketball team got its original name).

00:02:04.479 --> 00:02:07.640
“Seattle Supersonics win the game!”

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:11.310
But development stopped after a 1971 funding
cut.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:17.290
Russia’s effort, the Tupolev Tu-144, flew,
but it was grounded after an extraordinarily

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:23.940
spotty record over just 55 flights.
But there was a winner.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:30.870
“Concorde. The paper dart jet liner conceived
jointly by Britain and France to shrink the

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:36.349
world and cut air journey times in half.”

00:02:36.349 --> 00:02:41.319
From the beginning, Concorde was a marvel
of design. It wasn’t designed with computers,

00:02:41.319 --> 00:02:45.599
but through math and trial and error.
They had to innovate constantly to make a

00:02:45.599 --> 00:02:50.120
supersonic passenger plane possible.
“The airplane needed to be very long and

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.810
narrow to go supersonically comfortably.”

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:58.239
And the paint was twice as reflective as other
jets just to compensate for the heat from

00:02:58.239 --> 00:02:59.129
air friction.

00:02:59.129 --> 00:03:03.700
“Because you’re traveling at Mach II,
twice the speed of sound, even though you’re

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:07.090
at 60,000 feet...the airframe would actually
heat up, dramatically.”

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:10.840
“I actually touched the window. It wasn’t
warm, it was hot.”

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:15.819
Fuel flowed around the plane, during flight,
to adjust its center of gravity for takeoff,

00:03:15.819 --> 00:03:17.049
cruising, and landing.

00:03:17.049 --> 00:03:20.879
“So these pumps are working the whole flight,
but you can’t tell.”

00:03:20.879 --> 00:03:25.480
But it was the beautiful wing that distinguished
the Concorde for its greatest fans.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:29.569
“It’s a Delta Wing, but it’s called
an ogival delta wing because of its unique

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:30.349
shape.”

00:03:30.349 --> 00:03:35.849
Delta because it was triangular, like the
Greek Delta; ogival to reference its curve.

00:03:35.849 --> 00:03:40.799
The Delta Wing helped the Concorde get lift
at takeoff and limit drag while in flight.

00:03:40.799 --> 00:03:42.459
The rest of the plane compensated.

00:03:42.459 --> 00:03:48.799
“The one compromise in it, it required a
very high angle of attack at takeoff and landing.

00:03:48.799 --> 00:03:52.989
Since pilots couldn’t see out of the plane
because of angled landing, engineers put together

00:03:52.989 --> 00:03:53.939
a solution.

00:03:53.939 --> 00:03:56.310
“The Concorde featured a droop snoot.”

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:57.409
Droop snoot?

00:03:57.409 --> 00:03:59.180
“The snoot would droop.”

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:00.250
The snoot drooped.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:04.370
“The reason being that it was a Delta wing
design and had a very high angle of attack

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:09.209
on landing. So, in order to see, they were
able to lower the nose.”

00:04:09.209 --> 00:04:15.310
It flew at Mach 2 - more than 1300 miles per
hour — faster than the earth spins.

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:19.799
“You couldn’t tell — the only way you
knew your were doing Mach Two was that they

00:04:19.799 --> 00:04:24.750
had a Mach meter up on the bulkhead. Everybody
was focused on that, because it would creep

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:28.030
up. As soon as it went to Mach One, everyone
would break out into applause.”

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:32.720
To minimize drag, it soared so high you could
see the Earth’s curve.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:36.360
The Concorde defined the glamor of high speed
flight:

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:40.810
“Now this is a very important part of the
seven piece wardrobe, this washable dress

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:45.880
that she wears in hot climates”
And the admiration of celebrities like...Sting.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:52.810
“It’s always exciting flying Supersonic
and it’s always exciting to get to New York

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:56.870
before you’ve left.”
It was a stratospheric cocktail party.

00:04:56.870 --> 00:05:00.840
“Normally people complain about how bad
the airline food is...I will attest, in this

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:05.190
case, that was not true. This was one of the
best meals I ever had. It worked beautifully

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:11.870
— a normal French meal takes 2.5, 3 hours
— by the time dinner was over, we were here.”

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:14.080
So what went wrong?

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:20.660
On July 25, 2000, the Concorde punctured a
tire during takeoff for Air France Flight

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:26.830
4590. 113 people died.
Though failure happened shortly after takeoff,

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:30.319
it was due to a problem specific to Concorde
tires.

00:05:30.319 --> 00:05:36.180
The plane was grounded, until November of
2001. By that time, the September 11th attacks

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:41.100
had already depressed the industry.
But while both tragedies did affect Concorde,

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:46.780
they’re only a couple of pieces of the fundamental
challenges for the plane.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:49.280
Noise levels on takeoff were high.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:55.699
But massive sonic booms had no comparison.
In the 60s, the Air Force a ran a test of

00:05:55.699 --> 00:06:01.139
sonic booms over Oklahoma City, and residents
reported hundreds of damaged windows and noise

00:06:01.139 --> 00:06:04.840
disturbances.
All that meant limiting supersonic flight

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:08.750
to above the ocean — there would be no New
York to LA Concorde.

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:14.000
That’s part of what quashed the American
supersonic experiment with Boeing, and it

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:17.250
limited demand for supersonic planes from
the beginning.

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:20.910
Noise concerns were paired with environmental
concerns.

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:24.710
“There will be severe environmental damage
to the ozone layer.”

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:30.229
The plane’s high flight pattern made scientists
think its exhaust gas could be more threatening

00:06:30.229 --> 00:06:34.919
to the ozone than normal jets.
“What was noticeable was that you kept climbing,

00:06:34.919 --> 00:06:40.699
and climbing, and climbing. We were flying
much higher than a normal airliner.”

00:06:40.699 --> 00:06:46.340
A massive fleet of supersonic planes probably
would have caused real damage, setting red

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:52.680
flags for a supersonic future.
Fuel requirements also limited range to Transatlantic

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:59.360
journeys, without any Transpacific cash cows.
It guzzled enough fuel that price fluctuations

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:02.259
could hit particularly hard.

00:07:02.259 --> 00:07:07.520
With ticket prices as high as $12,000 a seat,
that was a significant risk.

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:13.740
And tickets had to be expensive, since at
most only 120 passengers could fit on the

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:16.620
plane. It couldn’t distribute the price
tag.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:21.190
That was compounded by the need for specially
qualified crewmembers and maintenance that

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:24.930
came at a premium. And it was all for a very
demanding crowd.

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:32.910
“Air France and British Airways had to position
a spare Concorde in New York in case the flight

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:37.910
had any problems. So there’s airplanes sitting
on the ground, not making any money, just

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:43.919
in case. Because Concorde passengers expect
to walk onto a Concorde because they paid

00:07:43.919 --> 00:07:47.949
a lot of money to it.”
None of these factors stopped the Concorde,

00:07:47.949 --> 00:07:52.129
but they all boxed it in until it had nowhere
to go but down.

00:07:52.129 --> 00:07:57.470
When Air France and British Airways announced
the Concorde’s closing on April 10, 2003,

00:07:57.470 --> 00:08:02.680
it wasn’t about past, but the future.
The manufacturer, Airbus, decided supporting

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:07.240
the Concorde was impossible.
An aging Concorde — it still had analog

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:12.960
controls and a flight engineer, both of which
newer planes had lost — would cost too much

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:18.020
to upgrade or redo.
In a way, Concorde economics were similar

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:23.080
to this toy model’s economics.
I got it for ten bucks because the manufacturer

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:28.830
could distribute the cost of factory workers,
tooling, and distribution over thousands of

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:33.070
cute planes.
Airbus loves doing the same with its family

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:38.980
of jets. Even if a flight were profitable
for an airline, the airline couldn’t afford

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:44.390
a new small batch of planes.
All the factors that boxed in Concorde kept

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:51.800
its scale so small, it would be wildly unprofitable
to service, rebuild, or revive.

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:59.600
The best option was to land for good.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.820
We like to think breakthroughs only end because
of disaster.

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:06.640
With a crash.
But they can fall short without disaster,

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:08.220
despite a breathtaking wing

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:12.770
or a jaw-dropping droop snoot.
They have to come with a business model and

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:17.580
supply system, a political resolve, and a
plan to expand.

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:23.320
Even as future dreams for Supersonic transport
still simmer, all those business model questions

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:26.510
remain unanswered.
“They don’t exist unless they make money...some

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:31.020
people don’t like that idea, but it’s
a fact of life. They’re there to make money

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:37.110
If they’re making a product that doesn’t
make money, they’ll stop making it or go

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:42.110
out of business. Or both. You never know.”
So the flight time to London can return to

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:48.480
a double feature slog.
But we lose something with the drudgery. Progress...slows.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:56.920
And we have to wait for something else to
look up at. Something worth pointing at.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:01.830
So I have fallen completely in love with the
Concorde, but it was not that comfortable

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:07.270
of a ride. Bob told me that while the legroom
was pretty good, the headroom was not and

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:11.480
neither was the seat width — it was kinda
like a coach seat. And you can see that in

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:17.090
this video of Sting. He looks pretty cramped 
— especially for Sting.

